    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A sultanate on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a>, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Sennar</a> from 1504 to 1821, that took the place of the Christian Nubian kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alodia">Alodia</a>.</li><li>Its wealth came from gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves traded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, and its sultans ruled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> lords such as the Abdallab.</li><li>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Funj</a> rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and Arabic spread across the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> orders and holy men, shaping modern Sudanese identity.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Scottish traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-bruce">James Bruce</a> passed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Sennar</a> in 1772 on his way back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and left one of the few eyewitness accounts of the Funj court.</p></aside>
    </section>

