    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/soba"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/soba.jpg" alt="Soba" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/soba">Soba</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dried_soba_noodles_by_FotoosVanRobin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FotoosVanRobin, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Funj Sultanate, known from its capital as the Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Sennar</a>, arose on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a> at the beginning of the sixteenth century. The origins of the Funj themselves are uncertain: they appear in the sources as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>-keeping people of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gezira">Gezira</a> and the upper Blue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, and theories linking them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shilluk-people">Shilluk</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> or to Umayyad refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> remain unproven, the last being a later genealogy that bolstered the dynasty’s Muslim credentials. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-chronicle">Funj Chronicle</a> and later tradition, their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amara-dunqas">Amara Dunqas</a> founded Sennar in 1504, a date accepted by most historians though it rests on late sources. Around the same time, the Christian Nubian kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alodia">Alodia</a>, long in decline, collapsed. Its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soba">Soba</a>, near the confluence of the Blue and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Niles</a>, was destroyed by an alliance of the Funj and the Arab tribes of the Qawasma led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-jamma">Abdallah Jamma</a>, whose descendants, the Abdallab, ruled the northern part of the kingdom from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qerri">Qerri</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of Sennar. The exact sequence of these events and the date of Soba’s fall are debated. By the middle of the sixteenth century the Funj had accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and their sultans ruled over the Gezira between the two Niles and, through the Abdallab, over the Nile valley northward towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-cataracts">Third Cataract</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/suakin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suakin.jpg" alt="Suakin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/suakin">Suakin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suakin,el-Geyf_mosque.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bertramz, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultanate rested on farming along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and in the rain-watered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gezira">Gezira</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a> was the staple crop, and on the herds of the Arab and Beja pastoralists of the plains. Its wealth, however, came largely from long-distance trade. Gold from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fazughli">Fazughli</a> and the borderlands with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, civet, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrich-feather">ostrich feathers</a> and, above all, enslaved people captured in raids on the peoples of the southern and western frontiers were sent north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> by caravan or east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suakin">Suakin</a>, in exchange for cloth, metal goods, weapons and luxuries. The sultans long controlled much of this trade and distributed imported goods to their nobles, a system that weakened in the eighteenth century as private merchants grew rich in towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shendi">Shendi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Sennar</a>. Sennar itself became a large market town, visited by the French physician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-jacques-poncet">Charles-Jacques Poncet</a> in 1699 and by the Scottish traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-bruce">James Bruce</a> in 1772. Religious life was shaped by holy men, scholars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> teachers; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadiriyya">Qadiriyya</a> order was introduced in the late sixteenth century, and families of holy men received land and privileges. Their lives were later collected by Muhammad al-Nur wad Dayfallah in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaqat-wad-dayfallah">Tabaqat</a></em>, the chief source for the period, completed in the early nineteenth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/iyasu-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iyasu-ii.jpg" alt="Iyasu II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/iyasu-ii">Iyasu II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iyasu_II_of_Ethiopia_and_Mentewab.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unnamed, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Funj</a> army was based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> of the nobility, armoured horsemen with lances and swords, supported by levies supplied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> lords. Firearms remained rare until late in the sultanate’s history. In the 1550s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, having conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, pushed south into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>, and a clash with Ottoman forces at Hannik, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-cataracts">Third Cataract</a>, probably in the later sixteenth century, fixed the long-term frontier between the two powers. Around 1611–1612 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adlan-i">Adlan I</a> defeated and killed the Abdallab ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajib-al-kafuta">Ajib</a>, who had rebelled, at Karkoj, reasserting Sennar’s supremacy over its northern vassals. Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badi-ii-abu-diqn">Badi II Abu Diqn</a> (r. 1644–1681) campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shilluk-people">Shilluk</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a> and made the hill kingdom of Taqali in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuba-mountains">Nuba Mountains</a> tributary. The captives from these campaigns were settled in villages around Sennar and formed a slave army loyal to the sultan, a force that later sultans used against their own nobility. In 1744 Funj forces defeated an invasion by the Ethiopian emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyasu-ii">Iyasu II</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinder-river">Dinder River</a>, and in the late 1740s the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abu-likaylik">Muhammad Abu Likaylik</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kordofan">Kordofan</a> from its Musabba’at rulers. Kordofan was lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate</a> in 1785, and by the early nineteenth century the sultanate’s forces were divided and weak.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/halfaya"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/halfaya.jpg" alt="Halfaya" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/halfaya">Halfaya</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%85%D8%AF%D9%8A%D9%86%D8%A9_%D8%AD%D9%84%D9%81%D8%A7%D9%8A%D8%A7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ayham.Alkasim, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Sennar</a> was a sacred ruler surrounded by elaborate ritual, and Funj royal customs, including the possibility that a ruler could be deposed and put to death by his own officials, show strong continuity with older Nilotic and Nubian kingship rather than with Islamic models. The sultanate was a hierarchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> lords, many of them bearing the title <em>manjil</em> or <em>manjilak</em>, who ruled their own districts, collected tribute and sent part of it to Sennar. The Abdallab shaykhs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qerri">Qerri</a>, later at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halfaya">Halfaya</a>, were the most important of these, governing the northern Arab tribes. The sultan’s control of the long-distance trade and of the distribution of luxury goods bound the nobility to the court, and marriage between the royal family and noble lineages reinforced these ties. Islamic law was administered by judges and scholars, but customary practice remained strong, and holy men often mediated between rulers and subjects. From the reign of Badi II, sultans relied increasingly on slave soldiers and officials rather than the old nobility, provoking resistance. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badi-iv">Badi IV</a> (r. 1724–1762) was deposed in 1762 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abu-likaylik">Muhammad Abu Likaylik</a>, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamaj">Hamaj</a>, a people of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gezira">Gezira</a>, Hamaj regents governed in the sultans’ name and installed or removed sultans at will until the end of the state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/khartoum"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khartoum.jpg" alt="Khartoum" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C2%A9Nile-Project_-Sari_Omer_0025.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Evon2023, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Funj</a> period transformed the religious and cultural character of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. When the sultanate was founded, Christian Nubian traditions still survived in the region; by its end, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and Arabic were dominant in the northern and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-sudan">central Sudan</a>, spread by holy men, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> orders and scholars who settled under Funj protection and founded schools for teaching the Qur’an. Many families of holy men whose authority dates from this period remained socially and politically important in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> into the twentieth century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadiriyya">Qadiriyya</a> and other orders introduced under the Funj became central to Sudanese religious life. The adoption of Arab genealogies by many riverain groups, which shaped later notions of Sudanese identity, also took shape in this period. The sultanate linked the Nile valley to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and the Sahelian kingdoms through trade, and Sennar was the chief political centre of the region between Egypt and Ethiopia for three centuries. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaqat-wad-dayfallah">Tabaqat</a></em> of Wad Dayfallah and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-chronicle">Funj Chronicle</a> are among the most important works of Sudanese Arabic literature. The Funj also bequeathed a political geography, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gezira">Gezira</a> and the confluence of the Niles, that the Turco-Egyptian rulers adopted when they made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a> their capital.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Muhammad Ali of Egypt" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali of Egypt</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ModernEgypt,_Muhammad_Ali_by_Auguste_Couder,_BAP_17996.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Auguste Couder, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultanate’s decline began in the eighteenth century as the rise of a slave army and a class of private merchants weakened the old balance between sultan and nobility. In about 1718 the nobles deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Unsa III and raised a new line of rulers, and the deposition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badi-iv">Badi IV</a> in 1762 left real power with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamaj">Hamaj</a> regents. Their rule was marked by repeated civil war between rival regents and princes, and outlying provinces broke away: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kordofan">Kordofan</a> fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> in 1785, while in the north the Shaigiya confederation and other groups along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> had long acted independently. By the early nineteenth century the authority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Sennar</a> barely extended beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gezira">Gezira</a>. In 1820 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali</a>, the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, sent an army under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-kamil-pasha">Ismail Kamil Pasha</a> to conquer the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, seeking gold, slaves for his new army and the elimination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> refugees who had settled at Dongola. Equipped with firearms and artillery, the Egyptian army defeated the Shaigiya at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korti">Korti</a> in November 1820 and advanced up the Nile. In June 1821 Badi VII submitted at Sennar without fighting, ending the sultanate. The Funj lands became part of the Turco-Egyptian Sudan, which in the 1820s and 1830s developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>, at the confluence of the Niles, as its capital.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Funj Sultanate of Sennar</a> ruled the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> for over three centuries after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alodia">Christian Alodia</a>, growing rich on gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slave trading, and under its rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, Arabic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> orders became dominant in the northern and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-sudan">central Sudan</a>, shaping the country’s religious and cultural identity.</em></p>

